---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Panel from '../../components/Panel.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Compliance as infrastructure — Somasuntaram">
  <Panel title="Overview">
    <h1>Compliance as infrastructure</h1>
    <p>
      "Compliance" covers two genuinely different things, and conflating them
      is how this kind of claim falls apart under questioning. One is
      technical controls that exist as code — I either wrote the Terraform/IAM
      that implements them or I didn't. The other is a formal certification
      audit — a much bigger scope than infrastructure alone, where my
      contribution was real but partial. This page keeps those separate on
      purpose instead of blending them into one undifferentiated "I did
      compliance" claim.
    </p>
  </Panel>

  <Panel title="Controls I implemented directly">
    <p>
      Four specific controls on a Kubernetes platform, each something I wrote
      or configured myself — not a framework name, just what the code
      actually does:
    </p>
    <div class="callout-good">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Resource-scoped IAM, not wildcards.</strong> The Secrets
          Manager access policy is scoped to a single secret's ARN rather than
          a wildcard resource, and cluster add-ons (like the load balancer
          controller) run under IRSA — a per-service-account IAM role — rather
          than a shared node-wide role with broader permissions than any one
          workload needs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Secrets out of <code>.env</code> files.</strong> Application
          configuration moved from per-host <code>.env</code> files to AWS
          Secrets Manager, pulled into pods via the Secrets Store CSI driver —
          so a secret lives in one managed place with its own access policy,
          not copied across every host that happens to run the service.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Control-plane audit logging, actually retained.</strong>
          EKS control-plane logging enabled for audit, authenticator, and
          controller-manager log types, with a 90-day retention policy set
          explicitly rather than left at CloudWatch's indefinite default —
          a deliberate retention decision, not an accident of not configuring
          it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Transport encryption on the logging cluster itself.</strong>
          The Elasticsearch cluster backing centralized logs runs with a
          custom CA and per-node TLS certificates for both HTTP and
          inter-node transport — the logging pipeline doesn't get a pass on
          encryption just because it's internal infrastructure rather than a
          customer-facing service.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p>
      None of this is framed as "ISO 27001" or "PCI DSS" — no certification
      audit ever reviewed this specific environment, so I'm not claiming one
      did. These are controls an auditor or a skeptical interviewer could
      point at in the code and ask "does this actually do what you say," and
      the answer holds up.
    </p>
  </Panel>

  <Panel title="Formal certification: SOC 2 Type II">
    <p>
      Separately: a SOC 2 Type II audit came back with
      <strong>no exceptions noted</strong> — including a control citing
      CIS-benchmark-based system hardening as evidence. That's a real,
      external, adversarial review outcome, not a self-assessment.
    </p>
    <p>
      My part in it was narrower than "I did SOC 2": access-management
      coordination and keeping evidence current, plus responding to security
      questionnaires that referenced the audit once it existed. I didn't own
      the audit engagement, and I'm not claiming the technical controls in
      the section above were literally the evidence cited in this specific
      audit — they're from separate work. What I can defend is the
      coordination work and the outcome itself, stated as what it was.
    </p>
  </Panel>

  <Panel title="Where the two connect">
    <p>
      The reason both belong on the same page: they're the same underlying
      idea applied at different scopes. A certification is only as credible
      as the technical controls underneath it — access scoped by IAM policy
      instead of by convention, secrets in a managed store instead of a
      config file, logs that are both captured <em>and</em> retained on
      purpose, encryption that isn't skipped because a system is "just
      internal." The controls section is what that looks like in Terraform
      and Kubernetes manifests. The audit section is what it looks like when
      an outside party checks whether all of it actually holds, end to end —
      not just the pieces one person can point to.
    </p>
  </Panel>

  <Panel title="Next steps">
    <p>
      The natural next step is enforcing encryption at rest in code
      everywhere, as a policy check rather than a convention, so it's a
      guarantee and not just a direction. And "coordination" on the SOC 2
      side is deliberately general. I'd rather keep it
      that way than imply ownership of an audit scope — user access reviews,
      vendor risk, policy documentation — that other people carried most of.
    </p>
  </Panel>
</BaseLayout>
